trajectory.patch:
The `MANY` macro, which is intended to represent `CONFIG_TEST_LIB_HASH_MAP_MAX_ENTRIES`, is used in several test files (`get.c`, `insert.c`, `load_factor.c`, `remove.c`). The Kconfig help text explicitly states that `CONFIG_TEST_LIB_HASH_MAP_MAX_ENTRIES` "translates to MANY in the test sources."

The `_main.h` file, which would typically contain such a common definition, is not provided in the project source. This suggests that `MANY` is currently undefined when these test files are compiled. An undefined macro, when used in an expression, can sometimes lead to unexpected behavior or be implicitly treated as `0` by the preprocessor, or if used as a variable, it could be an uninitialized variable with a garbage value. A garbage value for `MANY` would cause loops to iterate an extremely large number of times, leading to memory exhaustion or out-of-bounds access within the hash map operations, resulting in the observed `Segmentation fault`.

To fix this, `MANY` must be explicitly defined using the Kconfig value `CONFIG_TEST_LIB_HASH_MAP_MAX_ENTRIES`. Since `_main.h` is not provided for modification, the definition must be added to each C file that uses `MANY`. All relevant C files already include `zephyr/ztest.h`, which transitively includes `zephyr/autoconf.h`, making `CONFIG_TEST_LIB_HASH_MAP_MAX_ENTRIES` available as a macro.

